          "message": {
            "text": "Implementation-coupled patch: `patch.object(database.time, \"sleep\")` only works if `database.py` does `import time` (so `database.time` resolves to the time module). If the module instead uses `from time import sleep`, the patch target is missing and the test will raise `AttributeError` from `patch.object` itself, masking the actual behavior under test. Conversely, if a future refactor changes the import style, the retry-delay assertion (`sleep.assert_called_once_with(0.0)`) silently loses its meaning. Either (i) confirm the import style and document the dependency, or (ii) patch the `time` module globally (`patch(\"time.sleep\")`) which works regardless of how `database` imports it."
